<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow"><title>Paper A · Learner paper</title><base href="/api/v1/ap-chinese/2027/a/file/"><link rel="stylesheet" href="paper.css"><body><main class="page teacher"><section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">HSKKit · AP Chinese 2027 · Original practice</p><h1>Paper A · Learner paper</h1><p class="lead">55 MCQs + four free-response tasks. Speaking uses an independent music-research scenario; the official 2027 project packet is not yet released.</p><div class="links"><a href="index.html">Practice workspace</a><a href="student.pdf">Learner PDF · 简体</a><a href="student-traditional.pdf">Learner PDF · 繁體</a><a href="teacher.pdf">Answer PDF</a><a href="listening.html">Audio review</a></div></div></section><div class="box"><h2>Before speaking: prepare your own research</h2><p>Before starting the speaking section, investigate how a musical practice in a Chinese-speaking community connects people. Use at least two identifiable sources, distinguish documented facts from your own interpretation, and prepare brief notes in your own words. The two source leads below are starting points; consult the original pages and record any additional research. This is independent practice, not an official course-project submission.</p><p><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/guqin-and-its-music-00061">UNESCO: Guqin and its music</a></p><p class="cn">古琴是七弦弹拨乐器。联合国教科文组织的介绍把古琴与文人传统、较亲密的演奏环境联系起来。可以继续研究：音乐在怎样的场合被聆听？这种场合如何影响演奏者与听众的关系？这里的文化解释应以资料支持，不能据此概括所有当代演奏。</p><p><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/nanyin-00199">UNESCO: Nanyin</a></p><p class="cn">南音与福建闽南以及海外闽南社群的文化生活有联系。联合国教科文组织的介绍提到泉州方言演唱、器乐与歌唱形式，以及婚礼、节庆等社会场合。可以继续研究：语言与集体演奏如何维系地方经验？不同社群是否会赋予同一音乐形式不同意义？</p></div><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 1</p><h2>Project presentation</h2><p>Present what you learned about how a musical practice in a Chinese-speaking community connects people. Describe a specific cultural practice or product, explain its relationship to a perspective or value, and support your explanation with your research. Include a comparison or reflection and acknowledge a limit in your evidence. Speak in Chinese.</p><p class="cn">请介绍你研究的一种华语社群音乐实践怎样连接人与社区。描述具体的文化实践或文化产物，解释它与某种观念或价值的关系，并结合研究证据。作适当比较或反思，也说明证据的局限。请用中文作答。</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 11 points maximum: 任务完成 2, 文化理解 2, 组织 2, 语言控制 3, 表达呈现 2. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 2</p><h2>Project Q&A</h2><p>Respond in Chinese to four questions about your research. Each prompt is played twice, followed by a forty-second response window. The four responses form one task and are reviewed together.</p><p class="muted">Four recorded questions. Each prompt plays twice, then allows 40 seconds for a response. Open the practice workspace or audio review to hear them.</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 9 points maximum: 任务完成 2, 文化理解 2, 语言控制 3, 流利度 2. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 3</p><h2>Story narration</h2><p>Write a complete story in Chinese using all four pictures in the order shown. Include a beginning, a development, and an ending. The pictures provide the situation; choose your own details and wording.</p><p class="cn">请按顺序使用全部四幅图，用中文写一个完整故事，包括起因、经过和结果。人物姓名与具体细节由你决定。</p><div class="story-grid"><figure><img src="assets/images/story-1.svg" alt="Volunteers prepare an outdoor book exchange; dark clouds approach."><figcaption>Picture 1</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-2.svg" alt="Rain begins; a volunteer shields books on the table while another notices the community room."><figcaption>Picture 2</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-3.svg" alt="Volunteers carry the boxed books into the community room together."><figcaption>Picture 3</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-4.svg" alt="Neighbors exchange and read dry books indoors; the volunteers are relieved."><figcaption>Picture 4</figcaption></figure></div><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 8 points maximum: 叙事展开 3, 组织 2, 语言控制 3. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 4</p><h2>Email response</h2><p>You received the email below from a community-center coordinator. Reply in Chinese, responding to all requests and using a suitable greeting and closing.</p><p class="cn">社区中心｜关于新居民交流活动
同学你好！我们想邀请你帮助策划一次让新搬来的居民认识邻里的活动。请推荐一种活动，说明为什么它适合不熟悉彼此的人，并介绍你可以负责什么。
有些居民不太熟悉中文，也有人只能在周末参加。你会怎样照顾这些需要？预算有限，如果不能购买新材料，你有什么替代办法？另外，请向我们提出一个你在准备前需要确认的问题。
谢谢！
协调员 林老师</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 8 points maximum: 信息展开 3, 组织 2, 语言控制 3. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L1 · Questions 1–5</p><h2>Choosing a neighborhood activity</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l1.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>周末社区活动安排</caption><thead><tr><th>活动</th><th>时间</th><th>地点</th><th>是否预约</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>旧物交换</td><td>周六 09:00–11:00</td><td>东院</td><td>不用预约</td></tr><tr><td>自行车检查</td><td>周六 14:00–16:00</td><td>西门</td><td>需要预约</td></tr><tr><td>阳台种植</td><td>周日 09:00–10:30</td><td>屋顶花园</td><td>需要预约</td></tr><tr><td>邻里故事会</td><td>周日 15:00–16:30</td><td>二楼阅览室</td><td>不用预约</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">本安排及数据为原创练习情境。</p><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l1.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>週末社區活動安排</caption><thead><tr><th>活動</th><th>時間</th><th>地點</th><th>是否預約</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>舊物交換</td><td>週六 09:00–11:00</td><td>東院</td><td>不用預約</td></tr><tr><td>自行車檢查</td><td>週六 14:00–16:00</td><td>西門</td><td>需要預約</td></tr><tr><td>陽臺種植</td><td>週日 09:00–10:30</td><td>屋頂花園</td><td>需要預約</td></tr><tr><td>鄰里故事會</td><td>週日 15:00–16:30</td><td>二樓閱覽室</td><td>不用預約</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">本安排及數據爲原創練習情境。</p></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>1. Which activity best meets Wang’s schedule and preference?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The item exchange in the eastern courtyard.</p><p><b>B.</b> The neighborhood story gathering in the reading room.</p><p><b>C.</b> The bicycle inspection at the western gate.</p><p><b>D.</b> The balcony gardening lesson on the roof.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>2. What changes if it rains during the gardening lesson?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The lesson moves to Sunday afternoon.</p><p><b>B.</b> Participants must reserve a different session.</p><p><b>C.</b> The activity takes place in the upstairs reading room.</p><p><b>D.</b> The venue changes to the first-floor lobby.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>3. What should a registered gardening participant bring?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> An empty flowerpot.</p><p><b>B.</b> A bag of prepared soil.</p><p><b>C.</b> A cash payment for materials.</p><p><b>D.</b> A set of gardening tools.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>4. What can someone without a bicycle reservation do?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Join an inspection if they arrive before opening.</p><p><b>B.</b> Pay to obtain an additional inspection place.</p><p><b>C.</b> Attend the safety talk without taking an inspection place.</p><p><b>D.</b> Move their bicycle inspection to the gardening venue.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>5. Why does the speaker emphasize that the activities are free?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To announce that reservation requirements have been removed.</p><p><b>B.</b> To distinguish the price from the need to reserve some activities.</p><p><b>C.</b> To encourage residents to bring larger objects for transport.</p><p><b>D.</b> To indicate that the center will provide transport for exchange items.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L2 · Questions 6–10</p><h2>A river clean-up that changed direction</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l2.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l2.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>6. What is the report mainly about?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Replacing all volunteers with an automated cleaning system.</p><p><b>B.</b> Ranking visitors by the amount of waste they produce.</p><p><b>C.</b> Testing ways to reduce waste by investigating its source.</p><p><b>D.</b> Closing a riverside path until every piece of litter is removed.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>7. Why is the concentration of bottles downstream not enough to identify who dropped them?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Some bottles may have floated there from upstream.</p><p><b>B.</b> The downstream bend has no space for visitors to stop.</p><p><b>C.</b> The volunteers remove all upstream rubbish before counting.</p><p><b>D.</b> The report says all drink containers are sold downstream.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>8. Which measure would make the comparison between the two entrances fairer?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Counting only bottles that volunteers personally saw being dropped.</p><p><b>B.</b> Adding the total number of rubbish bags from both entrances.</p><p><b>C.</b> Asking volunteers to clean the busier entrance for longer.</p><p><b>D.</b> Comparing the amount of litter per hundred visitors.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>9. How does the team address concern about time spent collecting data?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It suspends rubbish collection for the rest of the month.</p><p><b>B.</b> It divides observation and cleaning between rotating teams.</p><p><b>C.</b> It limits observations to periods when no visitors use the path.</p><p><b>D.</b> It drops the comparison and returns to bag rankings.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>10. What attitude does the team take toward an early reduction in rubbish?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It treats any reduction as proof of permanent success.</p><p><b>B.</b> It dismisses the trial because cup returns take effort.</p><p><b>C.</b> It regards the reduction as a result requiring further checks.</p><p><b>D.</b> It assumes returned cups must already have been reused.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L3 · Questions 11–15</p><h2>Keeping an interview in the speaker’s voice</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l3.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l3.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>11. What solution do the speakers favor for listeners unfamiliar with the dialect?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Retain selected original audio and add explanations.</p><p><b>B.</b> Replace the resident’s speech with a student’s recording.</p><p><b>C.</b> Remove all language that needs additional explanation.</p><p><b>D.</b> Publish the entire interview without any editing.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>12. Why should the resident hear the edited version before publication?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To make sure each excerpt uses standard Mandarin.</p><p><b>B.</b> To select the loudest parts of the recording.</p><p><b>C.</b> To confirm that editing has not changed the intended context.</p><p><b>D.</b> To decide which language is superior for a public tour.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>13. What is the likely benefit of asking what an old place name brings to mind?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It makes a factual list of names unnecessary for all future research.</p><p><b>B.</b> It invites the resident to connect the name with personal experience.</p><p><b>C.</b> It ensures the answer can be given without using any dialect.</p><p><b>D.</b> It directs the resident toward agreeing with the students’ opinion.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>14. Which statement best represents the male speaker’s view of dialect and Mandarin?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A dialect should always replace Mandarin in public communication.</p><p><b>B.</b> Mandarin is suitable only for explaining unfamiliar place names.</p><p><b>C.</b> One language must be chosen before the interview can be recorded.</p><p><b>D.</b> Different forms of speech can serve different situations.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>15. What will the final trial listening help the students check?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Whether listeners distinguish the resident’s words from added commentary.</p><p><b>B.</b> Whether the resident remembers every street name used in the past.</p><p><b>C.</b> Whether the recording can be extended to thirty minutes.</p><p><b>D.</b> Whether two classmates can imitate the resident’s dialect accurately.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L4 · Questions 16–20</p><h2>Contributing a reliable local observation</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l4.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l4.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>16. What should a participant do before relying on the automatically detected location?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Move to the second row of trees.</p><p><b>B.</b> Delete any earlier observations nearby.</p><p><b>C.</b> Turn off all location information in the project.</p><p><b>D.</b> Check that the location corresponds to the tree observed.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>17. What is the purpose of photographing a branch from a similar angle on later visits?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To make all tree species appear identical.</p><p><b>B.</b> To make changes easier to compare over time.</p><p><b>C.</b> To avoid having to record the date of a visit.</p><p><b>D.</b> To prove the automatic location can never be wrong.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>18. What should someone do when they cannot identify a tree confidently?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Enter the most common species found on campus.</p><p><b>B.</b> Leave the project until an expert can visit in person.</p><p><b>C.</b> Mark the identification as uncertain rather than guess.</p><p><b>D.</b> Reuse the species name from an unrelated photograph.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>19. When should a record flagged as a duplicate be removed?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> When comparison confirms it is an identical accidental submission.</p><p><b>B.</b> Whenever it describes the same tree as any previous record.</p><p><b>C.</b> Whenever two different people submit observations on the same day.</p><p><b>D.</b> When the system notices the location is near another tree.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>20. Why does the speaker mention a tree that has not flowered?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To discourage participants from visiting outside the flowering season.</p><p><b>B.</b> To suggest that incomplete records should be removed.</p><p><b>C.</b> To recommend recording only trees expected to flower soon.</p><p><b>D.</b> To show that absence of flowering is meaningful observation data.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L5 · Questions 21–25</p><h2>Repairing an object, keeping a memory</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l5.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l5.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>21. What is the presentation’s central point?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Older objects are always safer and cheaper than new ones.</p><p><b>B.</b> Decisions about keeping objects involve different kinds of value.</p><p><b>C.</b> A technical specialist should decide what every owner must preserve.</p><p><b>D.</b> Objects without a family story should always be discarded.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>22. Why is Liu uncomfortable when the radio’s casing looks too new?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> She believes a newer casing makes the news harder to hear.</p><p><b>B.</b> She wants to sell the radio as a recently purchased model.</p><p><b>C.</b> She objects to all repairs regardless of their effect.</p><p><b>D.</b> The old appearance is connected to the memories she values.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>23. How did the survey change the proposed repair day?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Owners would first discuss which changes and traces mattered to them.</p><p><b>B.</b> Only objects that could be repaired for free would be accepted.</p><p><b>C.</b> Repairers would restore every object to a new-looking condition.</p><p><b>D.</b> Participants would stop discussing costs and safety concerns.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>24. What does the speaker suggest when an electrical object is unsafe to use?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Use it only during family gatherings to reduce the danger.</p><p><b>B.</b> Ask its owner to disregard all memories connected with it.</p><p><b>C.</b> Find a way to preserve its meaning without operating it.</p><p><b>D.</b> Let the age of the object determine whether safety rules apply.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>25. Why does the speaker mention the small number of interviewees?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To claim that six opinions are enough to represent everyone.</p><p><b>B.</b> To limit how broadly the survey’s findings can be generalized.</p><p><b>C.</b> To argue that interviews cannot reveal any useful information.</p><p><b>D.</b> To explain why all participants had the same reason for keeping objects.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R1 · Questions 26–30</p><h2>A community music exchange</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 上周征求意见时，几位社区居民说，他们很想听学生演奏，却担心从头到尾坐着听，自己没有参与的机会。我们因此把原定的舞台演出改成“音乐故事交流会”。活动仍在下周五下午举行，地点是社区活动室，不再使用学校礼堂。</p><p><b>p2</b> 前半小时，三位居民会带来对自己有特别意义的歌曲，讲讲第一次听到这些歌的经历；同学们可以提问。后半小时，由我们演奏短曲，并邀请大家试打节奏。陈阿姨愿意拉二胡，不过她希望先和学生见个面，商量怎样搭配，避免两边各演各的。</p><p><b>p3</b> 请大家在周三前选好一首不超过三分钟的曲子，并回复两三句话，说明为什么选它、想向居民问什么。我们不是要把旧歌照搬过来，也不是只追求新奇，而是想让不同的生活经历在音乐里找到联系。如果需要改编，请先问清楚歌曲的来源，以及带来这首歌的人是否介意。</p><p><b>p4</b> 另外，活动室离住户很近，不能使用扩音设备；预算也只够租几把椅子。请不要用报名人数或音量来判断活动是否成功。活动结束后，我们会请居民和同学各写下一件从对方那里听来的新鲜事。能不能真正听见彼此，比节目排得多热闹更重要。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 上週徵求意見時，幾位社區居民說，他們很想聽學生演奏，卻擔心從頭到尾坐著聽，自己沒有參與的機會。我們因此把原定的舞台演出改成「音樂故事交流會」。活動仍在下週五下午舉行，地點是社區活動室，不再使用學校禮堂。</p><p><b>p2</b> 前半小時，三位居民會帶來對自己有特別意義的歌曲，講講第一次聽到這些歌的經歷；同學們可以提問。後半小時，由我們演奏短曲，並邀請大家試打節奏。陳阿姨願意拉二胡，不過她希望先和學生見個面，商量怎樣搭配，避免兩邊各演各的。</p><p><b>p3</b> 請大家在週三前選好一首不超過三分鐘的曲子，並回覆兩三句話，說明為什麼選它、想向居民問什麼。我們不是要把舊歌照搬過來，也不是只追求新奇，而是想讓不同的生活經歷在音樂裡找到聯繫。如果需要改編，請先問清楚歌曲的來源，以及帶來這首歌的人是否介意。</p><p><b>p4</b> 另外，活動室離住戶很近，不能使用擴音設備；預算也只夠租幾把椅子。請不要用報名人數或音量來判斷活動是否成功。活動結束後，我們會請居民和同學各寫下一件從對方那裡聽來的新鮮事。能不能真正聽見彼此，比節目排得多熱鬧更重要。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>26. Which statement best summarizes the revised event plan?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A student concert will raise funds for a larger performance space and a new set of instruments.</p><p><b>B.</b> Residents will judge student arrangements to decide which version best preserves the character of an older song.</p><p><b>C.</b> Students and residents will share performances and personal memories in an event designed to bring them together.</p><p><b>D.</b> Students will introduce new music while residents listen without taking part in the activities or discussions.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>27. What should students include in their reply to Lin Yue?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Their chosen piece, the reason for choosing it, and a question they would like to ask residents.</p><p><b>B.</b> A resident’s favorite piece, a written account of its history, and permission from the music teacher.</p><p><b>C.</b> An estimate of attendance, a list of equipment needed, and a plan for covering the rental costs.</p><p><b>D.</b> A recording of their rehearsal, the names of all performers, and the order of the entire program.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>28. In paragraph 2, what does “各演各的” most nearly mean?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Choosing musical styles from different generations to make the program more varied.</p><p><b>B.</b> Performing separately without planning how the two groups’ contributions will fit together.</p><p><b>C.</b> Playing the same piece more than once so that listeners can compare versions.</p><p><b>D.</b> Preparing their parts individually before combining them at a later shared rehearsal.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>29. Which proposed addition would best fit the goals and limits described in the email?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Move to the school auditorium and spend the remaining funds on a longer student-only showcase.</p><p><b>B.</b> Use one familiar song for every performance so that the musicians can skip their planning meeting.</p><p><b>C.</b> Add amplified introductions so that neighbors who stay at home can also follow the activities.</p><p><b>D.</b> Let mixed groups discuss a song’s memories, then create a short rhythm quietly together.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>30. Why does Lin Yue ask students to check a song’s background and the resident’s feelings before changing it?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To decide whether the song is old enough to deserve a place in the final program.</p><p><b>B.</b> To ensure that changes respect the meaning the song has for the person who shares it.</p><p><b>C.</b> To prevent residents from hearing unfamiliar versions and keep the event entirely focused on older music.</p><p><b>D.</b> To make every performance follow the same arrangement so that its technical quality can be compared fairly.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R2 · Questions 31–35</p><h2>A library tries a different booking rule</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青禾图书馆试行座位预约后，管理人员发现，预约成功并不意味着座位被充分使用。有些读者一次预约三小时，实际上只坐半小时；也有人临时想来，却在页面上看见“已满”。管理员因此选了两个条件相近的阅览区，比较两种做法。</p><p><b>p2</b> 甲区维持原规则，每人最多预约三小时，迟到半小时才释放座位。乙区把单次上限改为九十分钟，迟到十五分钟释放；如果无人排队，读者可以续约。两个区域的座位数量和开放时长相同。下表是四周试行期间每天的平均数据，不是全市读者调查。</p><p><b>p3</b> 乙区的空置情况减少了，但满意度并没有随之提高。一些需要长时间阅读的读者说，反复续约让人分心。管理员打算保留较快释放空位的做法，同时增加少量可预约较长时间的座位。她强调，数据帮助我们看见问题，却不能替读者说明所有感受。下一轮除了记录数字，也会安排访谈，了解不同读者的需要。</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>两个阅览区的四周试行记录</caption><thead><tr><th>指标</th><th>甲区</th><th>乙区</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>每天平均预约次数</td><td>80</td><td>112</td></tr><tr><td>已预约时间中的空置比例</td><td>25%</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>受访读者满意比例</td><td>78%</td><td>70%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">原创模拟数据。满意比例来自各区试行期间的受访者，不代表所有读者。</p><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青禾圖書館試行座位預約後，管理人員發現，預約成功並不意味着座位被充分使用。有些讀者一次預約三小時，實際上只坐半小時；也有人臨時想來，卻在頁面上看見“已滿”。管理員因此選了兩個條件相近的閱覽區，比較兩種做法。</p><p><b>p2</b> 甲區維持原規則，每人最多預約三小時，遲到半小時才釋放座位。乙區把單次上限改爲九十分鐘，遲到十五分鐘釋放；如果無人排隊，讀者可以續約。兩個區域的座位數量和開放時長相同。下表是四周試行期間每天的平均數據，不是全市讀者調查。</p><p><b>p3</b> 乙區的空置情況減少了，但滿意度並沒有隨之提高。一些需要長時間閱讀的讀者說，反覆續約讓人分心。管理員打算保留較快釋放空位的做法，同時增加少量可預約較長時間的座位。她強調，數據幫助我們看見問題，卻不能替讀者說明所有感受。下一輪除了記錄數字，也會安排訪談，瞭解不同讀者的需要。</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>兩個閱覽區的四周試行記錄</caption><thead><tr><th>指標</th><th>甲區</th><th>乙區</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>每天平均預約次數</td><td>80</td><td>112</td></tr><tr><td>已預約時間中的空置比例</td><td>25%</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>受訪讀者滿意比例</td><td>78%</td><td>70%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">原創模擬數據。滿意比例來自各區試行期間的受訪者，不代表所有讀者。</p></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>31. What problem prompted the trial?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Readers were reserving seats in a different city.</p><p><b>B.</b> The library had more seats than it could maintain.</p><p><b>C.</b> Readers were unable to access any reservation website.</p><p><b>D.</b> Reserved time was sometimes unused while others were excluded.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>32. Which conclusion is supported by both the article and the table?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Longer sessions always produce higher satisfaction for every reader.</p><p><b>B.</b> Better use of reserved time does not necessarily mean greater satisfaction.</p><p><b>C.</b> More reservations prove that the library needs fewer interviews.</p><p><b>D.</b> The new rules increased the percentage of reserved time left empty.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>33. What is the difference between the two areas’ unused proportions?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> 15 percentage points.</p><p><b>B.</b> 15 percent of all library seats.</p><p><b>C.</b> 35 percentage points.</p><p><b>D.</b> 10 percent of all registered readers.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>34. How would the planned adjustment respond to the trial’s mixed results?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> By returning every seat to the original three-hour rule.</p><p><b>B.</b> By removing reservations and relying only on arrival order.</p><p><b>C.</b> By keeping faster release while offering some longer sessions.</p><p><b>D.</b> By requiring every reader to renew at fifteen-minute intervals.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>35. Why does the author describe the scope of the table’s data?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To show that every city follows the same reservation policy.</p><p><b>B.</b> To prove that interviews always give unreliable information.</p><p><b>C.</b> To demonstrate that the experiment lasted an entire year.</p><p><b>D.</b> To prevent readers from treating a local trial as a universal survey.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R3 · Questions 36–40</p><h2>A riverbank that leaves room for change</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青湾河边新修了一段步道，开放后却没有立刻得到一致好评。一位居民说，栏杆旁为什么还留着杂草和倒下的树枝？既然花钱改造，就应该收拾得整整齐齐。设计小组没有马上把这些东西清走，而是在现场组织了一次步行讨论。</p><p><b>p2</b> 生态调查员解释，规划中有一小段是“自然观察带”。一些枯枝给小动物提供藏身处，几种看似不起眼的草能留住土壤。保留它们不等于完全不管理：挡住通道的树枝必须移开，影响安全的地方也要及时处理。观察带与主步道之间留有清楚边界，方便人们选择行走路线。</p><p><b>p3</b> 居民并非都被说服。有家长担心孩子走进去，也有轮椅使用者指出，一处提示牌正好挡在坡道旁。小组原先准备在网上发一篇长说明，后来决定先移动提示牌，再请居民共同画出需要改进的路段。他们发现，有些争论源于对生态的不同理解，有些则是确实存在的使用障碍。</p><p><b>p4</b> 三个月后，观察带没有被变成花坛，但增加了简明标识，入口也更容易辨认。项目负责人说，公共空间不必在“全都人工整理”和“完全放任不管”之间二选一。让人理解保留的理由，同时愿意修改不方便的设计，才能让不同需要在同一条河边共存。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青灣河邊新修了一段步道，開放後卻沒有立刻得到一致好評。一位居民說，欄杆旁爲什麼還留着雜草和倒下的樹枝？既然花錢改造，就應該收拾得整整齊齊。設計小組沒有馬上把這些東西清走，而是在現場組織了一次步行討論。</p><p><b>p2</b> 生態調查員解釋，規劃中有一小段是“自然觀察帶”。一些枯枝給小動物提供藏身處，幾種看似不起眼的草能留住土壤。保留它們不等於完全不管理：擋住通道的樹枝必須移開，影響安全的地方也要及時處理。觀察帶與主步道之間留有清楚邊界，方便人們選擇行走路線。</p><p><b>p3</b> 居民並非都被說服。有家長擔心孩子走進去，也有輪椅使用者指出，一處提示牌正好擋在坡道旁。小組原先準備在網上發一篇長說明，後來決定先移動提示牌，再請居民共同畫出需要改進的路段。他們發現，有些爭論源於對生態的不同理解，有些則是確實存在的使用障礙。</p><p><b>p4</b> 三個月後，觀察帶沒有被變成花壇，但增加了簡明標識，入口也更容易辨認。項目負責人說，公共空間不必在“全都人工整理”和“完全放任不管”之間二選一。讓人理解保留的理由，同時願意修改不方便的設計，才能讓不同需要在同一條河邊共存。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>36. What is the article mainly arguing?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Ecological preservation and usable public space can be negotiated together.</p><p><b>B.</b> A riverbank must be cleared completely before residents can visit.</p><p><b>C.</b> Professional explanations make public feedback unnecessary.</p><p><b>D.</b> A natural observation area should replace every accessible path.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>37. Which action is consistent with the management of the observation strip?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Keeping every fallen branch regardless of where it lies.</p><p><b>B.</b> Replacing all wild plants with decorative flower beds.</p><p><b>C.</b> Moving a branch that blocks the walking route.</p><p><b>D.</b> Removing the boundary so that visitors must enter the strip.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>38. What does the misplaced sign reveal?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> All disagreement comes from a lack of ecological knowledge.</p><p><b>B.</b> Wheelchair users prefer longer online explanations to design changes.</p><p><b>C.</b> Signs are harmful wherever they are placed.</p><p><b>D.</b> Some complaints identify practical barriers rather than opposing conservation.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>39. Why did the team invite residents to map problem areas?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To transfer all maintenance responsibility to visitors.</p><p><b>B.</b> To gather specific feedback that could guide physical improvements.</p><p><b>C.</b> To prove that the original plan could never be changed.</p><p><b>D.</b> To identify complaints that could be settled simply by a longer online explanation.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>40. What does “二选一” refer to in the final paragraph?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Treating total tidiness and total nonintervention as the only alternatives.</p><p><b>B.</b> Choosing whether the river should serve children or older adults.</p><p><b>C.</b> Deciding whether to open the path in spring or summer.</p><p><b>D.</b> Selecting which resident will be responsible for all decisions.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R4 · Questions 41–45</p><h2>The folds of a paper map</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 搬家那天，我在抽屉里找到一张折得发白的公交地图。手机里明明有更快的路线，我还是把它摊在桌上。外婆用红笔画过一条线，从旧家门口一直绕到河边，旁边写着三个小字：“别着急。”</p><p><b>p2</b> 小时候，我总嫌外婆坐车慢。她宁可多走一站，也要在市场前下车，看看熟悉的摊主，再沿河走回家。我问她为什么不坐到楼下。她说：“到了地方，不一定就算走完了路。”那时我不懂，只觉得大人总能给麻烦找到理由。</p><p><b>p3</b> 新家离公司近了，窗外却没有我认识的人。第一个周末，我按地图上的红线坐回去。市场换了招牌，卖豆腐的阿姨已经不在原处。我有点失望，直到一个收摊的老人问我，是不是以前常跟着外婆来的那个孩子。他记不住我的名字，却记得我总把书包带拖在地上。</p><p><b>p4</b> 回程时，我没有删掉手机里的最快路线，也没有照着旧地图走完每一个弯。我把那张纸重新折好，留出红线的一角。原来有些路不是为了省下几分钟，而是让一个人知道，自己曾经怎样被这个地方认出来。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 搬家那天，我在抽屜裏找到一張折得發白的公交地圖。手機裏明明有更快的路線，我還是把它攤在桌上。外婆用紅筆畫過一條線，從舊家門口一直繞到河邊，旁邊寫着三個小字：“彆着急。”</p><p><b>p2</b> 小時候，我總嫌外婆坐車慢。她寧可多走一站，也要在市場前下車，看看熟悉的攤主，再沿河走回家。我問她爲什麼不坐到樓下。她說：“到了地方，不一定就算走完了路。”那時我不懂，只覺得大人總能給麻煩找到理由。</p><p><b>p3</b> 新家離公司近了，窗外卻沒有我認識的人。第一個週末，我按地圖上的紅線坐回去。市場換了招牌，賣豆腐的阿姨已經不在原處。我有點失望，直到一個收攤的老人問我，是不是以前常跟着外婆來的那個孩子。他記不住我的名字，卻記得我總把書包帶拖在地上。</p><p><b>p4</b> 回程時，我沒有刪掉手機裏的最快路線，也沒有照着舊地圖走完每一個彎。我把那張紙重新摺好，留出紅線的一角。原來有些路不是爲了省下幾分鐘，而是讓一個人知道，自己曾經怎樣被這個地方認出來。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>41. Why does the narrator return along the red route?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To prove the phone’s directions are technically incorrect.</p><p><b>B.</b> To persuade all former vendors to reopen their stalls.</p><p><b>C.</b> To reconnect with a place associated with the grandmother.</p><p><b>D.</b> To reach the office faster from the new apartment.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>42. How did the child initially understand the grandmother’s slower route?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> As an unnecessary complication justified by adults.</p><p><b>B.</b> As the only physically possible route to their home.</p><p><b>C.</b> As evidence that the market was closed to younger people.</p><p><b>D.</b> As a way to avoid meeting familiar neighbors.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>43. What is the significance of the older man remembering the schoolbag strap?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> He can identify every resident by full name.</p><p><b>B.</b> A small remembered detail restores the narrator’s sense of connection.</p><p><b>C.</b> He wants the narrator to buy a new schoolbag at the market.</p><p><b>D.</b> He proves that the market has remained completely unchanged.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>44. What does the narrator’s decision to keep both the phone route and the paper map suggest?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The narrator intends to abandon the new home immediately.</p><p><b>B.</b> Only an exact recreation of the past can preserve memory.</p><p><b>C.</b> Paper maps are always more accurate than digital navigation.</p><p><b>D.</b> Efficiency and a meaningful connection to place can coexist.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>45. How does the meaning of “别着急” develop across the story?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It becomes a warning that every bus on the route is unsafe.</p><p><b>B.</b> It turns into an instruction to finish moving more quickly.</p><p><b>C.</b> It comes to suggest allowing time for relationships and recognition.</p><p><b>D.</b> It means that the grandmother disapproved of every technological change.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R5 · Questions 46–50</p><h2>An invitation to repair, not replace</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青禾生活坊｜旧物新用体验日
周六 10:00–12:00，社区一楼工坊。费用为每人二十元，仅用于工具维护与基础耗材；不包括另购零件。十二个名额，请周四晚八点前预约。同行者如也要操作，须各自报名。</p><p><b>p2</b> 请带一件小型、非电器的旧物，例如开线的布袋、松动的木盒。我们不接收带电设备、尖锐破损玻璃或有化学残留的容器。不能确定物品是否合适？报名时附一张照片，工作人员会提前回复。未获确认的特殊物品请勿直接带来。</p><p><b>p3</b> 这不是把物品交给师傅、过一会儿来取的维修服务。导师会协助判断问题，参加者亲自尝试合适的步骤。没有经验也可以报名，但不保证每件物品当天修好。遇到需要专业设备的问题，我们会说明原因和后续选择，不勉强操作。</p><p><b>p4</b> 最后二十分钟是交流环节：请分享这件物品为什么值得留下，以及今天学到的一点经验。你也可以只倾听，不必公开私人故事。若临时不能来，请至少提前一天取消，让等候者有机会补位。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青禾生活坊｜舊物新用體驗日
週六 10:00–12:00，社區一樓工坊。費用爲每人二十元，僅用於工具維護與基礎耗材；不包括另購零件。十二個名額，請週四晚八點前預約。同行者如也要操作，須各自報名。</p><p><b>p2</b> 請帶一件小型、非電器的舊物，例如開線的布袋、鬆動的木盒。我們不接收帶電設備、尖銳破損玻璃或有化學殘留的容器。不能確定物品是否合適？報名時附一張照片，工作人員會提前回復。未獲確認的特殊物品請勿直接帶來。</p><p><b>p3</b> 這不是把物品交給師傅、過一會兒來取的維修服務。導師會協助判斷問題，參加者親自嘗試合適的步驟。沒有經驗也可以報名，但不保證每件物品當天修好。遇到需要專業設備的問題，我們會說明原因和後續選擇，不勉強操作。</p><p><b>p4</b> 最後二十分鐘是交流環節：請分享這件物品爲什麼值得留下，以及今天學到的一點經驗。你也可以只傾聽，不必公開私人故事。若臨時不能來，請至少提前一天取消，讓等候者有機會補位。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>46. Who is the event primarily designed for?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Customers seeking guaranteed same-day electrical repairs.</p><p><b>B.</b> People willing to try repairing suitable small objects themselves.</p><p><b>C.</b> Professional repairers looking for unrestricted workshop access.</p><p><b>D.</b> Visitors who want to leave an object and collect it later.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>47. A participant is unsure whether an unusual object is suitable. What should they do?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Bring it without notice so the tutor can decide during the session.</p><p><b>B.</b> Assume that paying the fee guarantees the object will be accepted.</p><p><b>C.</b> Ask a companion to register in their place without describing it.</p><p><b>D.</b> Submit a photo when booking and wait for staff confirmation.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>48. Which cost is not included in the twenty-yuan fee?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Basic consumable materials.</p><p><b>B.</b> Maintenance of shared tools.</p><p><b>C.</b> Additional replacement parts.</p><p><b>D.</b> Participation in the final discussion.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>49. Why does the notice state that private stories need not be shared?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To allow participation without pressuring people to disclose personal experiences.</p><p><b>B.</b> To prevent participants from listening to anyone else’s account.</p><p><b>C.</b> To require every participant to submit a written history instead.</p><p><b>D.</b> To show that an object’s personal value has no place in the event.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>50. What is the stated reason for cancelling at least one day in advance?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To obtain additional parts at no charge.</p><p><b>B.</b> To allow someone on the waiting list to take the place.</p><p><b>C.</b> To transfer the booking automatically to every companion.</p><p><b>D.</b> To postpone the entire event to another weekend.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R6 · Questions 51–55</p><h2>Permission to publish an oral-history clip</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 邮件一｜学生许宁致受访者周阿姨
周阿姨您好！谢谢您分享老街的故事。我们想把采访剪成三分钟短片，放在学校的公开网站上。片中会出现您谈到的旧门牌，以及您年轻时在街口拍的合影。我们准备只写您的姓，不写全名。请问您是否同意？如果有不适合公开的部分，请告诉我们。</p><p><b>p2</b> 邮件二｜周阿姨回复
许宁你好！我愿意让更多人了解这条街，不过照片里还有几位朋友，当年大家只答应私人留念，我不能替他们同意公开。请先不用那张合影。至于旧门牌，可以拍门牌本身，但不要拍到现在住户的门内。我的声音可以保留，最好先把剪好的片子发给我听一遍。有一句话是转述别人的猜测，不能剪得像是我亲眼见过的事。只写姓没问题，不过我希望页面说明这是个人回忆，不是经过全面核实的街道历史。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 郵件一｜學生許寧致受訪者周阿姨
周阿姨您好！謝謝您分享老街的故事。我們想把採訪剪成三分鐘短片，放在學校的公開網站上。片中會出現您談到的舊門牌，以及您年輕時在街口拍的合影。我們準備只寫您的姓，不寫全名。請問您是否同意？如果有不適合公開的部分，請告訴我們。</p><p><b>p2</b> 郵件二｜周阿姨回覆
許寧你好！我願意讓更多人瞭解這條街，不過照片裏還有幾位朋友，當年大家只答應私人留念，我不能替他們同意公開。請先不用那張合影。至於舊門牌，可以拍門牌本身，但不要拍到現在住戶的門內。我的聲音可以保留，最好先把剪好的片子發給我聽一遍。有一句話是轉述別人的猜測，不能剪得像是我親眼見過的事。只寫姓沒問題，不過我希望頁面說明這是個人回憶，不是經過全面覈實的街道歷史。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>51. Which use has Zhou explicitly declined for now?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Publishing the old group photograph.</p><p><b>B.</b> Including a recording of her voice.</p><p><b>C.</b> Showing the old street number by itself.</p><p><b>D.</b> Identifying her only by her surname.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>52. Why does Zhou want the edited clip sent to her first?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To remove every statement that differs from a published history book.</p><p><b>B.</b> To replace the entire interview with a written transcript.</p><p><b>C.</b> To check that a secondhand guess is not presented as firsthand evidence.</p><p><b>D.</b> To decide which school should host the public website.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>53. What distinction does Zhou make about consent?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A surname alone gives permission to publish any associated image.</p><p><b>B.</b> A student project never needs permission from anyone pictured.</p><p><b>C.</b> Permission for a private photo automatically includes public distribution.</p><p><b>D.</b> Her agreement does not authorize publication on behalf of other people.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>54. Which description would best meet Zhou’s requested framing of the clip?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A complete and verified history of every family in the street.</p><p><b>B.</b> One resident’s recollections, with uncertainty clearly identified.</p><p><b>C.</b> An anonymous official record requiring no further context.</p><p><b>D.</b> A reconstruction in which every secondhand account is treated as fact.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>55. How would you best describe Zhou’s overall response?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> She rejects every form of participation in the project.</p><p><b>B.</b> She accepts all proposed uses without any conditions.</p><p><b>C.</b> She supports sharing while setting specific boundaries.</p><p><b>D.</b> She agrees only if the website remains permanently private.</p></div></article></section></main></body></html>